WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.991
Domain 5 primer: Context Management & Reliability.

2
00:00:03.991 --> 00:00:12.788
Agents that run for many turns, across many sources, fail quietly unless you manage what they remember and when they hand off.

3
00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:16.993
Welcome to context management and reliability.

4
00:00:16.993 --> 00:00:23.516
The context window is everything the model can see at one moment: instructions, conversation, tool results.

5
00:00:23.516 --> 00:00:29.491
It is large but finite, and Anthropic's engineers note that recall gets less accurate as it fills.

6
00:00:29.491 --> 00:00:37.783
Reliability is the other half: failures should be visible, uncertainty should be honest, and humans should step in at the right moments.

7
00:00:39.183 --> 00:00:41.749
Think of three forces working against you.

8
00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:45.353
Loss, where summaries and long contexts blur exact details.

9
00:00:45.353 --> 00:00:49.141
Noise, where bulky tool output crowds out the important facts.

10
00:00:49.141 --> 00:00:55.190
And silence, where a hidden error or a missing source makes an answer look more certain than it is.

11
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.833
Every lesson in this domain is a technique for pushing back on one of these.

12
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:04.273
The six task statements cover the whole journey.

13
00:01:04.273 --> 00:01:06.743
Keep facts exact in long conversations.

14
00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:09.340
Escalate to people for the right reasons.

15
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.027
Pass errors up through multi-agent systems with enough context to recover.

16
00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:17.383
Explore large codebases without flooding the context.

17
00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:20.107
Decide where human review is really needed.

18
00:01:20.107 --> 00:01:25.553
And keep sources and disagreements visible when combining many inputs into one answer.

19
00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.258
Here are the words this domain uses most.

20
00:01:29.258 --> 00:01:32.351
Pause the video and read through them, then keep going.

21
00:01:33.751 --> 00:01:35.945
Carry these ideas into the lessons.

22
00:01:35.945 --> 00:01:41.649
Keep important facts outside the conversation, in fact blocks, scratch files and manifests.

23
00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:45.473
Use subagents so heavy reading happens in a separate context.

24
00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:50.488
Let errors and coverage gaps travel upward with context instead of disappearing.

25
00:01:50.488 --> 00:01:55.127
Judge quality slice by slice, and keep every claim attached to its source.

